/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-----------------------*\
  Solution thermo: CITRIC ACID dissolved in liquid WATER (arity-2, the
  aqueous-solution tier -- the molecular-solute sibling of
  parameters/solution/tartaricAcid-water.dat, written the same way and from the
  same work).

  PAIR data: a heat of solution is defined as `crystal -> species dissolved in
  WATER`, so it names a second species and lives HERE, keyed by the (solute,
  solvent) pair, never inside citricAcid.dat (data-governance golden rule,
  arity-2).

  The engine reads dHsoln for the dissolved-vs-crystalline enthalpy rung:

      h_aq(T) = Hf_crystal + dHsoln + INT_298^T cp_aq dT'

  The dissolved species is the HYPOTHETICAL non-dissociated H3Cit (Kochergina's
  own reference state, Table 4 row "H3Cit (sln, H2O, hyp. nondis)"): at the
  concentrations a food or wine stream carries, the three dissociation steps
  move the enthalpy by tens of J/mol, not kJ/mol (Kochergina p. 900: the second
  to fourth terms of eq. (8) sum to < 0.03 kJ/mol at the dilutions studied), so
  the neutral rung is the right first approximation and is stated as such.  The
  dissociated route is the citrate family (species/H3Cit, H2Cit, HCit, Cit;
  chemistry/*Cit-formation), see docs/design/organic-acids-in-the-catalogue.md.

  NOT carried: the aqueous heat capacity `Cp`.  With no `Cp` key the engine would
  integrate the cp_aq rung with the component's liquidHeatCapacity -- which
  citricAcid.dat does not carry, because no work in hand prints it.  The rung
  therefore refuses by name when a duty reaches the sensible heat of a citric
  solution; the formation-level (298.15 K) balance is complete.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

solute      citricAcid;
solvent     water;

// Standard enthalpy of solution: crystalline H3Cit -> aqueous (hypothetical
// non-dissociated, high dilution), 298.15 K.  ENDOTHERMIC (positive): dissolving
// citric acid cools the solution.  From ONE study on ONE material:
//   dHsoln = Hf(H3Cit, sln, hyp. nondis.) - Hf(H3Cit, cr)
//          = -1534.3 - (-1551.7)  =  +17.4 kJ/mol
// (Kochergina et al. 2006, Table 4).  The measured heats of solution at finite
// dilution, Table 2, run 18.8 kJ/mol (n = 9500 H2O per acid) to 17.4 (n = 900);
// the recommended hypothetical-state value is the extrapolation of eq. (8).
dHsoln      17400 J/mol;
dHsoln_provenance
{
    origin   literature;
    method   "Kochergina, Volkov, Krutov & Krutova, Russ. J. Phys. Chem. 80 (2006) 899-903, Table 4: Hf(H3Cit, sln, H2O, hyp. nondis., 298.15 K) = -(1534.3) kJ/mol and Hf(H3Cit, cr) = -(1551.7) +/- 1.3 kJ/mol (weighted mean of two calorimetric procedures) -> dHsoln = +17.4 kJ/mol.  Same study cited in citricAcid.dat for dHf_298, so crystal datum and heat of solution come from one material in one work.";
}
